<h2>The occurrence</h2>
<p>On the prosecution case, police patrolling Sector-5, Rohini in December 2010 acted on an informer's tip about illicit liquor sales. When they stopped a customer, one accused summoned associates and a scuffle erupted in which three constables were injured — one grievously, with a fractured nasal bone — before members of the public intervened. Recoveries included a baseball bat and brick stones, the bricks attributed to the appellant and a co-accused. The trial court convicted four of six accused in May 2025, sentencing the appellant to concurrent terms topping out at six years under Section 333 and four and a half under Section 308.</p>

<h2>The defence attack, and what landed</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Landed:</strong> the mens rea challenge to Section 308. The court found no prior enmity or premeditation, injuries mostly simple, and depositions that did not describe repeated brick blows — the material could not prove intent or knowledge of the homicidal kind beyond reasonable doubt.</li>
  <li><strong>Did not land:</strong> the Section 195 CrPC bar — the written complaint existed and was proved; contradictions over a shirt colour and a broken bat seal — held not to undermine the consistent attribution of brick assaults by three witnesses; the absence of independent public witnesses — explained by the setting; and the partial acquittals of co-accused — each case turned on its own attribution.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>How courts grade violence against public servants</h2>
<p>The judgment illustrates the layered scheme the IPC applied — and the BNS now carries forward — to obstruction and violence against public servants on duty: Section 186 for voluntary obstruction, Section 353 for assault or criminal force to deter from duty, Section 332 for voluntarily causing hurt, Section 333 for grievous hurt, and Section 308 only where the assault crosses into attempted culpable homicide. Each step up the ladder demands a distinct ingredient, and the gravest step demands a homicidal mental element that a spontaneous scuffle rarely supplies. The court's refusal to let the fracture alone carry Section 308 — while using Section 34 to sustain Section 333 — shows the two inquiries are different: common intention can distribute liability for the hurt actually caused, but it cannot manufacture an intent to kill that the evidence never showed.</p>

<h2>Fifteen years of pendency</h2>
<p>The appellant pressed mitigating circumstances — no prior conviction, sole breadwinner status, the case's decade-and-a-half life, compliance with bail conditions. The court's answer was structural rather than discretionary: it corrected the conviction to match the offence proved, leaving the lawful sentences for the sustained counts intact with concurrency and set-off. The judgment thus joins a consistent line of recent Delhi decisions — including the same judge's POCSO modification pronounced the same day — holding that appellate correction operates on the conviction first, and the arithmetic of sentence follows.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> In public-servant assault prosecutions, the charge under Section 308 IPC (Section 110 BNS) should be tested against four markers: premeditation, weapon and manner of use, situs and severity of injuries, and repetition of blows. Where all four point to a sudden, unplanned scuffle, the realistic exposure is the hurt ladder — Sections 332 and 333 (Sections 121(1) and 121(2) BNS) — and defence strategy, including on sentence and bail pending appeal, should be built on that footing.</p>
</div>
